      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Tesla Coil</strong><br>\nTesla invented the Tesla coil around 1891, which was crucial in the study of electricity. This device transforms energy into high-voltage charges, creating powerful electrical fields capable of producing spectacular electrical arcs. The Tesla coil has been used in radio technology, medical devices, and many other fields, and it continues to have applications in modern electrical engineering.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Alternating Current vs Direct Current</strong><br>\nTesla was a significant proponent of alternating current over direct current, which put him at odds with Edison. AC could be transmitted over long distances more efficiently than DC. The \"War of Currents\" ended with Tesla's AC system becoming the worldwide standard, which is still used today to transmit electricity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Work with Westinghouse</strong><br>\nGeorge Westinghouse, an entrepreneur and engineer, partnered with Tesla after becoming interested in AC. Tesla sold his patents to Westinghouse and worked with him to refine the AC system. Together, they worked on the first AC power plant at Niagara Falls, which successfully distributed electricity to Buffalo, New York.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
