          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Visionary Inventions</strong><br>\nTesla's work extended beyond electricity. He made discoveries in wireless communication, conducted experiments that contributed to the development of X-ray imaging, and even worked on a bladeless turbine. His interests and inventions were vast, including plans for a proposed wireless power system that could transmit electricity without wires globally.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Later Years</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Tesla lived in New York City, working on new inventions even as his fame and fortune began to fade. He became more eccentric and solitary, devoting himself to projects that often appeared fantastical, like a \"death beam\" which he claimed could end war. He died alone in his room on January 7, 1943, at the age of 86.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tesla's Legacy</strong><br>\nDespite dying impoverished, Tesla left a lasting legacy as an inventor and visionary. His work on AC electrical systems was his most significant contribution, powering homes and industries worldwide. Today, Tesla is remembered as one of history's great inventors, with his name living on in the Tesla electric car and the unit of magnetic field strength, the tesla.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
